            <ArticleTitle>Measured intelligence of problematic patients with chronic pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>939-43</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>100 patients with chronic pain judged &quot;problematic&quot; by their referring physicians were administered the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Test-Revised. 38 obtained IQs of 85 or less while only 11 earned IQs of 101 or higher. While a number of confounds may have influenced these results, low intelligence appears to be a marker for negative treatment prognosis, at least as perceived by physicians, and its influence would seem to merit further investigation and clarification.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
